14. Data Model

Each habit should roughly contain:

Habit
├── id
├── name
├── icon
├── color
├── frequency
├── createdAt
├── completedDates[]
└── reminder

The app should calculate:

Current streak
Best streak
Completion rate
Daily progress
Weekly progress

from the stored completion data.

15. Technical Requirements
Framework
React Native
Expo
Expo Router or React Navigation
Storage

Use local persistence such as:

AsyncStorage

No backend.

State

Use a simple state-management approach appropriate for a small app.

Components

Create reusable components such as:

HabitCard
ProgressCard
ProgressBar
Calendar
BottomTabBar
IconPicker
ColorPicker
EmptyState
Requirements
Responsive mobile layout
Android and iOS compatible
Expo Go compatible
No unnecessary dependencies
No authentication
No external API
Persist data after app restart
16. Design Direction

The visual style should be:

Modern + clean + friendly + premium

Avoid:

Overly futuristic UI
Neon colors
Excessive gradients
Excessive glassmorphism
Cluttered dashboards

Use:

Strong typography
Generous spacing
Rounded cards
Clear hierarchy
Subtle animations
Consistent iconography
Good empty states
Smooth completion interactions

The app should look like something that could realistically be published on the App Store / Play Store.

17. V1 Success Criteria

The app is considered complete when a user can:

Open the app.
Create a habit.
See it on the Home dashboard.
Mark it complete.
Have the completion persist after restarting the app.
See their streak update.
View historical completion.
Edit the habit.
Delete the habit.
View overall progress.
Change basic settings.